CSCE 1030- Project 1
Due: 10/02/2026 11.59 PM
Project Introduction
In this project, you will develop a complete, menu-driven Python application designed to
manage academic records, calculate GPAs, render formatted rosters, search student
profiles, and analyze institutional performance metrics.
Many real-world software applications—from legacy administrative systems to modern
terminal tools—rely on a central console loop to manage state in memory, manipulate data
structures, and execute user requests. This assignment serves as a comprehensive bridge
across Chapters 1 through 5, challenging you to apply core programming logic, structured
data handling, and input validation.
Your goal is to submit a fully functional, bug-free Python application that strictly adheres to
the technical constraints and functional specifications detailed below.
Project Requirements
1. Architectural & Language Constraints
To ensure all students demonstrate mastery of core control structures, your submission
must comply with the following structural rules:
• No Custom Functions (def Keyword Banned): You must NOT write custom
functions (def). All application logic—including menu navigation, calculations,
searches, and data manipulation—must exist inline within the main execution script
and its nested control structures.
• Persistent Execution Loop: The entire application must run inside a top-level while
loop driven by a state variable (e.g., system_active = True). The program must
continue running and returning the user to the main menu until option 0 is selected.
• Pre-Seeded Data Storage: To allow immediate testing without requiring manual
data entry on every launch, your program must initialize with pre-populated
collections containing at least 5 students.
• Pause-on-Return UX: At the end of every menu execution (options 1–5), the
program must pause using input("\nPress Enter to return to Main Menu...") before re-
displaying the main menu.
