3. Step-by-Step Menu Specifications
Your application menu must accept options '0' through '5'. Any input outside this range
must trigger an explicit error message ("[ERROR] Invalid choice...") without crashing the
application.
Menu Option 1: Input & Record Student Grades
1. ID Validation: Prompt for a 4-digit Student ID. Verify using .isdigit() and len() == 4. If
invalid, display a notification and exit back to the menu loop.
2. Name & Course Entry: Prompt for the student's full name. Prompt for a course
code, convert it to uppercase using .upper(), and add it to the enrolled_courses set.
3. Score Collection Loop: Ask how many assignment scores will be entered. Use a for
loop to prompt for each float score (0.0 – 100.0) and append them into a temporary
list.
4. State Update: If the ID is new, append it to student_ids and append the name to
student_names. Store or overwrite the grade list in student_grades[new_id]. Print a
confirmation message.
Menu Option 2: Compute GPAs & Academic Averages
1. Empty Check: Verify that student_grades contains data. If empty, notify the user.
2. Average Calculation: Loop through student_grades.items(). For each student,
compute sum(grades) / len(grades).
3. 4.0 Scale Mapping: Convert the numerical average to a standard 4.0 scale using
conditional branching:
o 90.0 or higher → 4.0
o 80.0 to 89.9 → 3.0
o 70.0 to 79.9 → 2.0
o 60.0 to 69.9 → 1.0
o Below 60.0 → 0.0
4. Letter Grade Evaluation: Use a for loop with enumerate() across
GRADE_BOUNDARIES to match the numeric average to its letter grade in
GRADE_LETTERS.
5. Persistence: Update student_gpas[sid] = gpa and output line item summaries for
each student formatted as: ID: 1001 | Avg Score: 88.8% | Letter: B | GPA: 3.0.
Menu Option 3: Display Formatted Grade Roster
1. Custom Aesthetics: Prompt the user to input a single border character (e.g., #, *, =).
Default to * if the user hits Enter without typing.
2. Table Header: Construct a centered ASCII header using string repetition and center
alignment (:^63).
3. Row Formatting: Iterate through student_ids using enumerate(student_ids).
Retrieve the corresponding name and GPA (student_gpas.get(sid, 0.0)).
4. Status Evaluation: Assign academic standing based on GPA:
o GPA 3.5 or higher → "Dean's List"
o GPA 2.0 to 3.4 → "Good Standing"
o GPA below 2.0 → "Probation Risk"
5. Tuple Packaging & Printing: Pack row items into a Tuple: (index + 1, sid, name, gpa,
status). Print each formatted line using precise width specifiers (e.g.,
{student_tuple[2]:<16}).
Menu Option 4: Search Student & Flag Academic Risk
1. Search Input: Prompt for a Student ID to look up (cast to int).
2. Linear Search Loop: Implement a manual lookup loop: for i in
range(len(student_ids)): to locate the target ID.
3. Record Inspection: If found, extract their name, grade list, and GPA. Display all
details.
4. Risk Classification: Apply logic to print an intervention flag:
o GPA below 2.0 → "[AT RISK] Student is on Academic Probation."
o GPA 3.5 or higher → "[EXCELLENT] Student made Honor Roll."
o Otherwise → "[NORMAL] Student is in Good Standing."
5. Not Found Handling: If the search loop finishes without a match, output "[NOTICE]
Student ID not found in system records.".
Menu Option 5: Class Statistics & High/Low Analysis
1. Data Aggregation: Initialize an empty master list all_scores = [].
2. Nested Unpacking Loop: Use nested for loops to iterate through every student's
score list and append every individual grade into all_scores:
Python
for sid, grades_list in student_grades.items():
for score in grades_list:
all_scores.append(score)
3. Statistical Analysis: Calculate:
o Total individual grades recorded (len(all_scores))
o Highest single score across all assignments (max(all_scores))
o Lowest single score across all assignments (min(all_scores))
o Overall class score average (sum(all_scores) / len(all_scores))
4. Summary Display: Print a formatted breakdown block with numbers formatted to 1
decimal place.
Menu Option 0: Exit Portal
• Set the control flag system_active = False, print a logout confirmation, and let the
script end cleanly.
Student Project Submission & Working Instructions
Academic Integrity Policy (Zero Tolerance)
• No External Help & No AI: This project must be 100% your team’s original work. The
use of generative AI tools (such as ChatGPT, Claude, GitHub Copilot, or similar
models), online code generators, contract coders, or external tutoring assistance is
strictly prohibited.
• Penalty: Any instance of cheating, plagiarizing code from external sources, or using
AI tools will result in an immediate grade of F for the entire course and will be
reported to the office of Academic Integrity.
• Oral Verification: TAs reserve the right to conduct random oral code reviews with
any student or group to verify full comprehension of the submitted code. If a student
cannot explain their implementation line-by-line, it will be flagged as an academic
integrity violation.
Project Execution & Server Environment
• Target Environment: You are free to use any platform, IDE, or operating system to
write your code (e.g., VS Code, PyCharm, IDLE, Mac, Windows, Linux). However,
ultimately your project will be compiled and tested on our official course
servers running Python 3. You are responsible for making sure your code runs
cleanly in that server environment without requiring local modifications.
• File Naming Convention: Name your Python submission file using the lead
submitter's Full Name, EUID, and Project 1:
o File Name Format: Firstname_Lastname_EUID_Project1.py
o Example: John_Doe_abc0123_Project1.py
• Execution Command: TAs will test and run your submission directly from the server
command line. Ensure your program executes cleanly using:
• External Libraries: Do not import external third-party packages (such as pandas,
numpy, or tabulate). Rely exclusively on Python’s standard library to ensure
compatibility with the server environment.
• Server Setup & Video Guide: I will post a video tutorial showing step-by-step how
to create your project, transfer your files, and test your code on the course server
before submitting. Watch this video and make sure your code runs on the server
well ahead of the deadline.
Group Formation & Collaboration Guidelines
• Group Size: You may complete this project individually or in a group of up to 4
students (1 to 4 members total).
• Lab Section Requirement: All members of a project team must be enrolled in the
exact same lab section. Cross-lab section groups are strictly prohibited.
• Group Flexibility: You are not locked into the same team for the entire semester—
you are free to change group members for future projects if you wish.
• Code Header & Contribution Overview (No Email Needed): You do not need to
email me your group details. If working in a group, use the submitter's information
for the file name, and include the full details of all team members along with a brief
overview of who worked on what inside the header docstring comment at the very
top of your script:
